course: adult-life
lesson: 41
title: Our street: who parked where and who won what
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every street has its news: who parked where, whose dog barked, who has a new car. Grown-ups love swapping it over the fence, and a lot of it is harmless fun. But a story passed along changes shape: a nip becomes «blood everywhere», four kittens become ten, and a guess becomes «everyone knows».
explain: A good way to sort any bit of gossip is to ask four things. What was actually seen? What has been blown up or guessed? What is private, the people's own business? And who could get hurt if the story spreads?
explain: Scene: in Leeds, Nan is at the window with her tea. «That Mr Hollis has parked in Number 9's space AGAIN. Third time this week!» What she saw is one car in one space on Tuesday. «Third time this week» is nobody's count, and why he parks there is his business until somebody simply asks him.
explain: Usually the facts are small and boring: a car, a van, an ambulance, a police car for twenty minutes. The exciting parts — the lottery win, the lost job, the «lad who was always trouble» — are what people add to explain the small facts.
explain: The kind fix is nearly always the same: a polite word, a knock, a wave, an offer of help — instead of a week of whispering.
fact: The confirmed part of a street story is what someone actually saw, like a car parked in a space on Tuesday.
fact: Words like «again», «every week», «always» and «everyone» often grow in the retelling; usually nobody counted.
fact: A new car and builders in the kitchen are facts; «they won the lottery» is a guess made up to explain them.
fact: How much money a family has, why someone is at home on a weekday, and who visits whom are private.
fact: «I'm not saying anything, but…» is still saying something; it hints at a story without any facts.
fact: Police at a door can mean a burglary, an accident, bad news or someone needing help; it never proves that a person did wrong.
fact: An ambulance outside a house tells you someone needed help, not who or why; offer help, not guesses.
fact: A couple's marriage is private, and there may be children in the house who hear what the street says.
fact: Comments about how someone's face or body looks turn into accusations; «you look well» is enough.
fact: A lonely old neighbour turned into a spooky legend needs a wave, not a story.
fact: Newcomers are busy and tired; the friendly thing is to say hello first instead of deciding they are «stuck up».
fact: Some gossip is a shared joke, like a gnome sending postcards from Spain; it stays fun while the people in it are laughing too.
fact: Grumbling about a noisy party is easier than knocking, but a knock and a kind word usually fix it.
fact: Before passing a story on, check it: a small nip can become a horror story in three tellings.
exercise: Nan: «Mr Hollis parked in Number 9's space AGAIN, third time this week!» What is actually confirmed? | His car was in that space on Tuesday | He parks there three times every week | He blocks the whole pavement every day | He is trying to start a row with Number 9
exercise: Mum: «The Dawsons won the lottery! New car, new kitchen!» Which part is a guess? | The lottery win | The new car on the drive | The builders in the kitchen | That they live at Number 4
exercise: Dad: «Fosters' party, music till three, someone singing Abba in the garden.» What's the kind fix if it keeps you awake? | Knock and ask them nicely to turn it down | Phone everyone on the street to complain about it | Play your own music louder back at them | Leave an angry note with no name on it
exercise: Auntie Bev: «Sandra's new 'friend', tall chap, smart shoes. I'm not saying anything, but…» Is she saying something? | Yes, she's hinting a story with no facts | No, she said she wasn't saying anything | No, she's only describing his shoes | Yes, she's proved who the man really is
exercise: Grandad: «7 and 9 are at WAR over the hedge, there's a tape measure involved!» What does «war» really stand for? | One cross talk and one letter | Years of fighting in the street | The police being called every week | Both families moving away for good
exercise: Kids: «Pearsons' dog bit the postman, blood everywhere, no post for our side now!» What really happened? | The dog nipped his hand through the letterbox | The dog bit his leg and he went to hospital | The post has stopped for good on that side | The dog chased him all the way down the road
exercise: Mrs Okafor: «New lot at 21, three vans, not one hello. Stuck up.» What might really be going on? | They're busy, tired or shy while unpacking | They have decided to hate the whole street | They have something to hide from everyone | They are rich so they won't talk to anyone
exercise: Mum, whispering: «Gary was out in his dressing gown at 11 on a Wednesday. Lost his job.» What's the honest version? | He was outside in his dressing gown, that's all | He has lost his job and is too sad to get dressed now | He has stopped going to work for good | He was sacked on Tuesday by his boss
exercise: Kayla: «Mrs Brennan's cat had TEN kittens and they're free!» What should you do before getting excited? | Ask the owner, there might be four and none spare | Run round with a box to fetch a kitten | Tell all your friends to go round and get one each | Ask the Pattersons for one from their shed
exercise: Dad: «Number 30's skip has been out six weeks, everyone's chucking rubbish in!» Who's actually harmed if people dump stuff in it? | The family who pays for the skip | The council who owns the road | The man who saw the fridge go in | Nobody, skips are for everybody
exercise: Auntie Jen: «Police at the Wilsons' last night! Always said there was something about that lad.» What's wrong with this? | Police come for lots of reasons, it proves nothing | Police only ever come when someone is going to be arrested | She's right, police never visit for nothing | Nothing, she saw the blue lights herself
exercise: Nan: «Morrisons are splitting up, his van's at his mum's every night.» Who could get hurt most if this spreads? | The Morrison children, hearing it from others | Nan, who saw the van with her own eyes | The man's mum, who has a guest staying | Nobody, it's only about a van
exercise: Mum laughing: «The Singhs' gnome sent a postcard from Spain!» Is this harmful gossip? | No, it's a joke the Singhs laugh at too | Yes, someone should call the police | Yes, the Singhs are being robbed | No, because gnomes really do travel
exercise: Big brother: «Old Mr Craddock never goes out, he's got hundreds of cats or something worse.» What is true? | He's old, stays in, and gets his shopping delivered | He keeps hundreds of cats in his house | He has a terrible secret hidden somewhere inside the house | He never eats because he never goes out
exercise: Auntie Bev: «Jackie's had something done to her face, nobody looks that good naturally.» What's the kind thing to say to Jackie? | «You look well!» and nothing more | «What have you had done to your face?» | «Bev says you've had your face done» | «You looked much older last year»
exercise: Dad: «Students at 17, sofa in the garden a month, living like a festival.» What's fact and what's picture? | The sofa is fact, the festival is a picture | Both are facts Dad has checked | The sofa is a joke, but the festival is a real fact | Neither one is anything but rumour
exercise: Mrs Okafor: «Hendersons' extension, I bet they never got planning permission.» What should she do first? | Check the council website, plans are public | Report them straight away to the council | Tell the whole street they broke the law | Knock down the bit that touches her fence
exercise: Kids: «Ice cream van man's a millionaire, five vans and a pool!» What's actually known? | The van comes on sunny days at four | He owns five ice-cream vans and a big swimming pool | He is the richest man in town | He sells ice cream to get famous
test: Your cousin texts: «nan says girl at no 5 dyed her hair BLUE and her mum is FURIOUS lol». Which bit is known? | The blue hair, the mum part's a guess | The furious mum, nan heard her shout | Both, nan saw it all from the window | Neither, nobody has seen the girl
test: Mum, on the doorstep: «Ambulance at the Lawsons' at six, hope it's not the baby.» What's the best thing for the street to do? | Offer help and wait for the family's news | Text round that the baby is very ill | Knock and ask what is wrong with the baby | Phone the hospital to find out more
test: Grandad: «Bins at no 2 out since Monday. EVERY week. Bloody foxes had a party.» Put him right. | One bad week isn't every week | He's right, they never take them in | It's fine to call them the bin people | The foxes are the real ones to blame
test: Auntie Jen: «Pam's son's back at his mum's, 32! Something must've gone wrong.» Which is the fair reply? | Lots of grown-ups move home for a while | Yes, he must have lost everything he ever had | Yes, he must have been thrown out | He's too old, he should be ashamed
test: Big sister, giggling: «man at 25 does karate in his garden at 7am IN HIS PANTS». They were shorts. What's the lesson? | Funny stories get sillier; don't add to them | It's fine to laugh at him to his face if he is silly | Shorts or pants, it makes no difference | You should stop him exercising outside
test: Your little brother says «Mr Craddock's house is haunted, he eats cats.» Put him right. | That's a made-up story; he's just old and lonely | It's true, the big kids saw it at night | Only half true, he just keeps the cats | Maybe, we should all go and look through his front window
test: Mrs Okafor: «Well if they're not gonna say hello, I'm not either.» What would change things? | Saying hello to them first | Waiting a year to see if they do | Asking around where they came from | Telling them the street's rules
test: Dad, cross: «The Fosters had a party till three!» Mum: «Was it three? I heard it stop at half twelve.» What happened to the story? | The time grew in Dad's telling | Mum is lying to protect the Fosters | The party really went on till six | Both of them are wrong, it was midnight
test: Kayla: «I'm gonna go ask for a free kitten!» Mum: «Nobody said they're giving them away.» Who could get bothered by this rumour? | Mrs Brennan, with kids knocking at her door | Nobody, kittens are always given away | The kittens, who will get too much food | The Pattersons, because it's their kitten
test: Auntie Jen: «Wilsons' lad, police round, told ya.» Later: the police came because the Wilsons had been burgled. What does it show? | Pinning a story on a person can hurt the innocent | Auntie Jen guessed right after all, as she usually does | The lad must have done something else | Police only visit bad families
test: Gary waves from the drive: «Night shifts, love — just got up!» What does this show about the street's story? | It was invented from a dressing gown | Gary is hiding that he lost his job | The street was right all along | Gary should not be seen outside like that
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the assertion that the Dawsons have won the lottery? | It is a conjecture formulated to account for observed purchases | It is an established fact confirmed by the Dawsons themselves | It is a matter of public record available from the council | It is a prediction concerning the Dawsons' future fortunes
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a police visit to a dwelling ought not to be attributed to wrongdoing by a particular occupant. | Such visits occur for numerous reasons, including the provision of assistance | Such visits are invariably undertaken in consequence of a crime | The police are not permitted to visit private dwellings | The occupants are obliged to explain every visit to the street
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dog belonging to the Pearsons. | The postman sustained a minor nip to the hand through the letterbox | The postman sustained a grave injury to the leg requiring hospital care | Postal deliveries to one side of the road were permanently withdrawn | The dog was removed from the household by the authorities forthwith
exam: It is given that a newly arrived household has not greeted its neighbours. Determine the most appropriate conduct on the part of an established resident. | To greet the newcomers first and offer a welcome | To refrain from greeting them until they do so first | To inform other residents that the newcomers are aloof | To make enquiries as to the newcomers' previous address
